 U('NERDC application: care of a motor vehicle','A suitable expository composition can cover regular checks of water/coolant, engine oil, brake fluid, tyres, brakes and servicing. Organise by safety and maintenance rather than merely listing materials. Explain the purpose of each action and end by linking regular care to safety, reliability and reduced breakdowns.'),
 U('Language and register','Match language to task. Narrative may use dialogue and vivid verbs; exposition values clarity and precision; argument uses reasoned persuasive language; description uses concrete sensory detail. Avoid texting abbreviations, uncontrolled slang and memorised “big words” that do not fit meaning.'),
 U('Editing checklist','Content: did I answer every part? Organisation: clear paragraphs and order? Expression: precise vocabulary and varied sentences? Mechanics: spelling, punctuation, capitals? Consistency: tense and person? Relevance: remove attractive sentences that do not serve the topic.'),
 U('Standard model paragraphs — four composition types','NARRATIVE — “The whistle sounded just as I reached the gate. I searched my bag again; the envelope was gone. For one second I wanted to run home, but the principal was already walking towards me.” Notice action, time movement and a developing problem. DESCRIPTIVE — “By noon the market seemed to breathe heat. Metal roofs flashed above rows of pepper and tomatoes while traders called prices across the narrow, crowded aisle.” Notice selected sensory detail and a controlled impression. EXPOSITORY — “Effective revision begins with a realistic timetable. A learner should divide large topics into smaller daily targets because specific tasks are easier to complete and review.” Notice a clear point followed by explanation. ARGUMENTATIVE — “Schools should teach responsible digital use rather than treat every phone as identical. Controlled access can support dictionaries and supervised research; however, clear rules are necessary because unrestricted use can distract learners.” Notice position, support and acknowledgement of an opposing concern. These are model paragraphs, not complete compositions: a full response develops several connected paragraphs around the same purpose.'),
 U('Mastery checkpoint','Given an unfamiliar prompt, you should identify the composition type, generate relevant points, make an outline, write a purposeful introduction, develop coherent body paragraphs, conclude appropriately, and revise for content, organisation and language.')
